   {:report
    "# Comparison Report: microFIT Contract Version 2.0 vs Version 3.1.1\n\nDate: 2026-04-26\n\n## Overview\nThis report compares the two provided microFIT contract texts:\n- Document A: FEED-IN TARIFF MICROFIT CONTRACT — Version 2.0 (23 pages)\n- Document B: FEED-IN TARIFF MICROFIT CONTRACT — Version 3.1.1 (22 pages)\n\nBoth documents share the same overall structure (Part 1 — Contract Details; Part 2 — General Terms and Conditions) and most clauses are word-for-word identical. The principal substantive changes between Version 2.0 and Version 3.1.1 are:\n- Removal of references to a Minimum Required Domestic Content Level and related Supplier obligations and disclosures (major change).\n- Addition of a requirement that certain notifications be made \"in the Prescribed Form\" (minor change).\n- Expanded disclosure language to permit sharing of facility details with other government bodies for public health, safety and emergency planning (privacy / disclosure change — moderate to major depending on privacy risk).\n- Version number and small editorial changes.\n\nBelow are detailed findings, per-section diffs, high-level key differences with impacts, and recommended next steps.\n\n## Key Changes (summary)\n- Domestic content requirement and related representations/covenants/disclosure references removed in v3.1.1 (major).\n- Notification format requirement changed in covenant (5.1.2) — now must notify \"in the Prescribed Form\" (minor).\n- Data disclosure clause expanded in v3.1.1 to explicitly allow sharing with other ministries/agencies for public health, safety and emergency planning (privacy impact — moderate).\n- Limited editorial changes and renumbering in Representations & Warranties (minor).\n\n## Detailed observations and implications\n- Removal of Minimum Required Domestic Content Level:\n  - Appears in Version 2.0 in Part 1 B(5)(iv), in Representations & Warranties (4.4.4), and is referenced in Collection/Disclosure of Information (6.5 & Disclosure subsection).\n  - In Version 3.1.1 those references are absent; the Domestic Content lines are omitted.\n  - Impact: alters supplier obligations and compliance requirements. Suppliers no longer warrant or covenant that a solar PV facility meets a specified domestic content threshold; OPA may no longer collect/verify domestic content compliance under this contract language. That is a material change to eligibility/compliance obligations and could affect program policy enforcement and supplier eligibility assertions.\n\n- Notification form:\n  - Version 2.0: 5.1.2 \"Supplier shall notify OPA promptly of any change to the information contained in Part 1, Section A(11).\"\n  - Version 3.1.1: 5.1.2 \"Supplier shall notify OPA promptly in the Prescribed Form of any change to the information contained in Part 1, Section A(11).\"\n  - Impact: administrative change that requires use of a specific form (may restrict informal notifications and standardize record-keeping). Minor but could require operational updates.\n\n- Expanded data sharing for public health/safety and emergency planning:\n  - Version 3.1.1 adds: \"The OPA may disclose certain information, including the details of the Facility and location of the Facility, to other government ministries, agencies, institutions or organizations, for purposes of promoting public health and safety and allowing emergency services personnel, as well as other public officials to plan and respond properly in the event of an emergency.\"\n  - Impact: expands permissible disclosure recipients and purposes beyond what is explicit in v2.0; increases privacy/data-sharing surface and may require additional supplier notice/consent considerations or internal data governance changes.\n\n## Recommendations\n1. Legal / Program Policy: Confirm whether removal of domestic content requirements reflects an intentional policy change. If not, advise reinstating the explicit Domestic Content warranty/obligation and related disclosure language.\n2. Suppliers: Suppliers previously relying on domestic content obligations (either to claim benefits or to meet program criteria) should be notified and advised on the practical effect; they should confirm whether program rules (outside the contract) still impose domestic content requirements.\n3. Privacy / Compliance: Review the expanded disclosure language with privacy officers to ensure compliance with applicable privacy laws and with OPA's privacy policy; update public privacy notices if necessary.\n4. Operations: Update intake and notification processes to implement \"Prescribed Form\" requirements; ensure forms and portals enforce the new requirement.\n5. If program administrators intend to retain the ability to audit domestic content or to collect evidence, consider adding an explicit transitional clause or standalone collection right.\n\n## Conclusion\nThe two contract versions are mostly identical in structure and wording except for a few important changes. The removal of domestic content language is the largest substantive change and has programmatic and compliance consequences. The added \"Prescribed Form\" requirement is operational. The expanded disclosure for emergency services increases privacy exposure and should be reviewed by privacy and compliance stakeholders.",
